:answer-field/id #uuid "812aa8aa-0438-595b-bab8-2d74de8d3b64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249894}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9801153f-b480-580e-a696-7b55c194e174" :question/math-academy-id "q-113983" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x+1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x+1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({u}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡x}{3}+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519b8fb0-5928-5ec9-8613-12c9d1306f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266873} {:answer/id #uuid "60890db1-c6f4-5143-9b9a-ae422da31c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266994} {:answer/id #uuid "87157abc-6212-562b-a343-90c57626ecf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267338} {:answer/id #uuid "8b99c65a-2714-56e5-af5f-38d5a8ea0940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267396} {:answer/id #uuid "919ec147-de01-530b-97cb-da754894b913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b99c65a-2714-56e5-af5f-38d5a8ea0940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267396} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c52e3dd-dd13-5927-892c-f499bebd302f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268655}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "996adb7b-cb39-5733-9d5d-ec3bcfe1a670" :question/math-academy-id "q-13663" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /6}2{csc}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /9$ | $\\pi /6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /6}2{csc}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2{csc}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c351233-a918-5f2c-867c-b36c3e634b45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288675} {:answer/id #uuid "6387ca6c-9af3-5305-93ac-6ba215b43157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288729} {:answer/id #uuid "6c47091c-3dbb-514d-926c-70024a19ee8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288795} {:answer/id #uuid "9c25054c-c830-5cdc-9739-35307de9de4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289083} {:answer/id #uuid "a683964a-9c75-50e4-b041-7650006dc260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a683964a-9c75-50e4-b041-7650006dc260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289134} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a9a7120-01c4-56ed-a4cb-cf7ba476dd9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289753}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "799e6798-92e1-543f-a4d9-7d772e9877f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-2203" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=10x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(10x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eb82788-efbe-50de-b226-6bde5d6aedf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb82788-efbe-50de-b226-6bde5d6aedf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317886} :answer-field/id #uuid "47ffe2b0-f398-575b-afe8-d12d64c3f4fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318580}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec7ce12f-cbf4-5c03-8053-383589db4d45" :question/math-academy-id "q-346368" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $2\\pi /3$ | $2\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u))\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =4(-1+2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9793ffe-67cc-5645-9b00-c140cc88e2aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9793ffe-67cc-5645-9b00-c140cc88e2aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318233} :answer-field/id #uuid "24f68f41-ba58-5d4c-9946-589591e9529a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318497}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8ccae74f-35c6-57d0-a03f-750b899cc692" :question/math-academy-id "q-350060" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}3{sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=x-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\pi /4$ | $0$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}3{sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{0}{sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{0} \\\\  & =3(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85c49c8e-61d5-5e58-a395-01a4e606b7d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85c49c8e-61d5-5e58-a395-01a4e606b7d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318020} :answer-field/id #uuid "773a7395-c2f8-5568-bc4a-ffe056234383" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318675}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d188146f-1019-54e0-bddf-9c317ac884d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-350071" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=x-\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /3$ | $2\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u){|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) \\\\  & =-4(1-2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde6a22f-af92-5d05-a00e-ee43025b38b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde6a22f-af92-5d05-a00e-ee43025b38b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429918} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b1c21c-bb6c-5c53-b4e6-f6635e6c2be4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429928}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66f3c3b9-c1e9-58f7-9604-16a02027760f" :question/math-academy-id "q-355818" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /20}^{\\pi /10}5{csc}^{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=5x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /20$ | $\\pi /10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /4$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /20}^{\\pi /10}5{csc}^{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}5{csc}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u){|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02192a18-96df-5c9c-86d1-fd39f10cc96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429909} {:answer/id #uuid "258b40a9-d7f7-5e38-ab76-8707b1a1a9b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429911} {:answer/id #uuid "59c2d43f-3033-53cf-a498-afe18808b478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429913} {:answer/id #uuid "605c38a6-bd94-5054-abeb-196bc585d8b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429915} {:answer/id #uuid "f513628d-4c08-512b-85ac-0d46df6f115b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02192a18-96df-5c9c-86d1-fd39f10cc96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429909} :answer-field/id #uuid "f070068b-56d2-50d4-88fa-ab05a0ca7204" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429929}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a6be6fa7-2d6e-5c8a-ab1e-bb035341f649" :question/math-academy-id "q-10578" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=x-\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /6$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡u{|}_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105573 :question/id #uuid "625c532a-d455-5b15-880d-e9fc5ae8ee72" :question/math-academy-id "e-374" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\left(\\dfrac 2 x\\right)\\, \\textrm{d} x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\dfrac 1 x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442 :topic/math-academy-id 43 :topic/title "The Constant Multiple Rule for Indefinite Integrals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690 :topic/math-academy-id 1116 :topic/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8760594-b3d4-5502-baee-afbaea7aec8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.t